----- 9c45b5d7154779bb3d5c7e946e49dd83f16b153b
Author: Georg Richter <georg@mariadb.com>
Date:   Mon, 19 Oct 2026 16:55:51 +0200

CONC-i860: Add STMT_ATTR_CB_RESULT_EX, a result callback with an end pointer

STMT_ATTR_CB_RESULT passes only the current position in the row to the
callback, so a callback cannot check that the data it parses is inside
the row. Add STMT_ATTR_CB_RESULT_EX with the signature

  int callback(void *data, unsigned int column, unsigned char **row,
               const unsigned char *end);

row points to the column's data (the length prefix for variable length
types) and is NULL for NULL values. end is the first byte after the
column's data. The callback must advance *row exactly to end. A non-zero
return value, or a *row different from end, aborts the fetch with
CR_MALFORMED_PACKET.

The legacy and the new callback share storage in MYSQL_STMT (union),
selected by a private type tag, so setting one replaces the other.
Passing NULL clears the callback and returns to the bind_result path.

mysql_stmt_attr_get() supports STMT_ATTR_CB_RESULT, STMT_ATTR_CB_RESULT_EX
and STMT_ATTR_CB_PARAM. The getter for the inactive result callback
returns NULL. Because older libraries reject unknown attributes,
mysql_stmt_attr_get(stmt, STMT_ATTR_CB_RESULT_EX, &cb) can be used to
detect support without side effects.

----- 9f326a3a87d5fe368de005d7bc271897fdbb413a
Author: Georg Richter <georg@mariadb.com>
Date:   Tue, 20 Oct 2026 17:16:51 +0200

CONC-i864: Fix memory leak in mariadb_dyncol_list_named()

The result array is allocated before the column names are read. When a
name cannot be read, the function returned ER_DYNCOL_FORMAT without
freeing the array and without resetting *names, so the memory was
leaked and the caller was left with a non-NULL pointer although the
function reported an error.

----- cffcd368b08f341e7d5e4fa5ed1033af6bad2131
Author: Georg Richter <georg@mariadb.com>
Date:   Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:05:48 +0200

CONC-863: Check that name offsets of dynamic columns do not decrease

read_name() computed the length of a column name as the difference of
two name offsets read from the packed column, without checking that the
next offset is not smaller than the current one. The subtraction wrapped
around, and mariadb_dyncol_list_named() passed the result as size to
memcpy().

